#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" :question/math-academy-id "e-9907" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161911 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908" :question/problem "![](images/09/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dba961d-e303-56ab-9252-ba2464cee1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266691} {:answer/id #uuid "4378e1d0-c8f3-5953-926f-a34f93bba17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266747} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9bc846-a28a-56c0-bdb0-e96f8341147b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267407} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e44ed-9088-5422-8faa-e5a47f1b611c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268006} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0a071-152b-5d66-8b17-d25aa4bb1b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0a071-152b-5d66-8b17-d25aa4bb1b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268347} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe6c093-26dc-5d7a-ada3-01470001afa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdd91aaf-b8dd-5936-93a2-ee3b24ba20f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139418" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fdb6b55-695e-5cf8-8f1e-4b21b778d321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159387} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1c5e78-3d75-50dd-9ee2-9aa11b86e817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159388} {:answer/id #uuid "1f04ffb5-cee8-5a3b-bb90-0968a2be6114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159389} {:answer/id #uuid "502d14b9-9906-53cb-8e29-99023deaee86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159390} {:answer/id #uuid "569848a8-0ecd-59f9-8e86-ab8604c6794e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159391}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf51719b-7660-5cd1-ba34-c0d83d844a72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159386}] :question/id #uuid "7f4eb209-024e-54c1-8d3d-a6d7fb7fadd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139491" :question/problem "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $7$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862d7336-1d68-5324-8a48-626b5ab3fe1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267328} {:answer/id #uuid "ace6192e-8c33-5978-a97c-18fb7456a6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267704} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc3d50-c939-57bb-abf8-9b83ef1957ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267955} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9a5c87-ce40-5fa9-a431-5e8054488051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267991} {:answer/id #uuid "edd6cf5d-5e7b-5bae-ba67-eac4418c2dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc3d50-c939-57bb-abf8-9b83ef1957ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267955} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c64cec0-1f04-5d87-8e4d-1ea5c1b48553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e48008ac-4acd-5d33-8b94-d523fa814086" :question/math-academy-id "q-139492" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e0290b-b214-51b9-ae5b-fc3e291433d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266285} {:answer/id #uuid "4d569cb3-e268-51d7-8e1f-47a0e048ffa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266830} {:answer/id #uuid "50423a6e-7974-582d-a76f-cac676cf369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266858} {:answer/id #uuid "67f7f843-8d99-5b2c-a258-bfa87e57387b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267065} {:answer/id #uuid "bccbe989-ecf7-53c3-8c04-5337fd9a0320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50423a6e-7974-582d-a76f-cac676cf369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266858} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82d9035-63ea-54b8-90da-3fbf50bd2ad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c2ac9a-8e8a-51d7-84bb-f51632a65f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-139526" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1c07e06-038d-5f20-b7aa-6a49d66cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383426} {:answer/id #uuid "089c44cb-1411-5b96-a011-181bffcdb31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383427} {:answer/id #uuid "0538b559-3b27-53cf-bf4b-40d7ba06c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} {:answer/id #uuid "7e08020a-0701-57c1-8997-c2b48f3e8f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383429} {:answer/id #uuid "f1836749-28a7-5fbd-bd3f-d78ad98340b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0538b559-3b27-53cf-bf4b-40d7ba06c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} :answer-field/id #uuid "352c429f-c09f-51de-981a-03fd6b374654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5c458b4-8436-5a2e-a1dd-0c56afc161a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139540" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157ee7b9-2a37-56af-a675-3b93033c19d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266326} {:answer/id #uuid "2316ce20-3b2d-54fa-8fb0-522d6f775571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :a