ric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={5}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d37c0db-13b5-52bb-83d3-8faa90522c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266966} {:answer/id #uuid "75699b1d-f04e-5fa1-95af-55c5afb9ba07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267178} {:answer/id #uuid "b814b6e0-56b8-5328-8eaf-fd45d69b0e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267794} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dc7b46-4f84-5c1a-839a-f1607e0d90af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267803} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cba08f-6beb-54fa-8719-44a905a27f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8dc7b46-4f84-5c1a-839a-f1607e0d90af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267803} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e90099c-985a-55fb-9078-f8b9af859f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce6f50bf-ae89-57dc-a707-1d28279d986e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139542" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d8c257-9208-59a3-b8ef-e84226b5d7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b5625-8f3f-5546-bd45-82c8dcf4f2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400766} {:answer/id #uuid "9f361d5f-db40-531a-8f20-5389f05b015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400767} {:answer/id #uuid "c82a5223-3bd0-5bad-9e6b-edd5899707f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400768} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a6a4f9-ba6e-5a5b-9c4a-a88207909379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d8c257-9208-59a3-b8ef-e84226b5d7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dac3c89-e776-5135-b836-fafd4c581933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "925e17f0-1f91-564c-b150-3d5633a84c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139546" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e6efca-95b5-5f7f-84df-c43898904f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266499} {:answer/id #uuid "508bf2fb-93f6-5990-ad55-30bb910e8138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266862} {:answer/id #uuid "6ded4070-afc4-5321-930c-7fff89731796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267108} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c6b982-581f-5c4d-a40d-daef264ebab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267735} {:answer/id #uuid "c94c857c-1f07-525f-b49b-1f0a26b0f150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/