e corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$"}]}
